        <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Might is corrected as the intended weaker stance; must is rejected." data-hint="Listen for the contrastive stress on might and then must." data-fix="Might is corrected as the intended weaker stance; must is rejected."><p class="quiz-question">5. What does contrastive stress do in the final line?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Might is corrected as the intended weaker stance; must is rejected.">Might is corrected as the intended weaker stance; must is rejected.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="It changes testing into a finished past event.">It changes testing into a finished past event.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="It proves every modal always carries stress.">It proves every modal always carries stress.</button></div></div>
      </div>
      <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
    </div>
    <details class="reveal-card">
      <summary>Reveal the listening evidence</summary>
      <div class="reveal-body">
        <ol>
          <li>The reception record is the observation. It gives the later inferences an evidence base.</li>
          <li><span class="ex">Must</span> marks a strong positive inference; Studio A carries the key location information.</li>
          <li><span class="ex">Might</span> and <span class="ex">could</span> keep two explanations open. Each modal links into base <span class="ex">be</span>.</li>
          <li><span class="ex">Can’t</span> rejects Studio B because the current checked facts contradict that location.</li>
          <li>Contrastive stress repairs the reasoning strength: <span class="ex">might</span> is intended and <span class="ex">must</span> is explicitly rejected.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </details>
  </section>

  <h2>Read a complete evidence update</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Eli’s 2:11 p.m. fictional update</h3>
    <p><span class="ex">Reception marked Rina present at 2:07, and her labeled equipment case is beside Studio A. Two staff members heard a microphone test from that area at 2:09, so she must be nearby. She might be testing the wireless microphone inside, or she could be checking its signal from the hallway. She can’t be in Studio B because a staff member checked that room at 2:10 and found it empty and locked. Rina has not answered one call, but that does not prove she is unavailable. She might not have her phone with her. Eli is walking to Studio A to verify the conclusion.</span></p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You recovered the converging positive evidence, two open explanations, direct contradiction, and deliberately tentative phone conclusion from Eli’s note.">
    <h3>Reading check: use only the written evidence</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Her recent check-in, labeled case, and the test sound all point to that area." data-hint="The first two sentences provide three separate clues before must." data-fix="Her recent check-in, labeled case, and the test sound all point to that area."><p class="quiz-question">1. Why does Eli say Rina <span class="ex">must be nearby</span>?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Her recent check-in, labeled case, and the test sound all point to that area.">Her recent check-in, labeled case, and the test sound all point to that area.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A rule requires every person to remain near Studio A.">A rule requires every person to remain near Studio A.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Eli can see Rina directly inside the studio.">Eli can see Rina directly inside the studio.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="She might be testing inside, or she could be checking the signal from the hallway." data-hint="The third sentence keeps two locations and activities open." data-fix="She might be testing inside, or she could be checking the signal from the hallway."><p class="quiz-question">2. Which two explanations remain possible?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="She might be testing inside, or she could be checking the signal from the hallway.">She might be testing inside, or she could be checking the signal from the hallway.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="She is definitely at home or in Studio B.">She is definitely at home or in Studio B.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="She has finished a test that happened last year.">She has finished a test that happened last year.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="A staff member checked Studio B and found it empty and locked." data-hint="Find the because-clause after can’t." data-fix="A staff member checked Studio B and found it empty and locked."><p class="quiz-question">3. What evidence rules out Studio B?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A staff member checked Studio B and found it empty and locked.">A staff member checked Studio B and found it empty and locked.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina’s case has a printed label.">Rina’s case has a printed label.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="One phone call was not answered.">One phone call was not answered.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="One unanswered call does not eliminate other explanations." data-hint="The final sentences explicitly preserve the missing-phone possibility and require verification." data-fix="One unanswered call does not eliminate other explanations."><p class="quiz-question">4. Why does Eli avoid saying Rina <span class="ex">can’t</span> be available?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="One unanswered call does not eliminate other explanations.">One unanswered call does not eliminate other explanations.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Can’t is never used for present deductions.">Can’t is never used for present deductions.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Might not always means a confirmed negative fact.">Might not always means a confirmed negative fact.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Build one open possibility and one rejected explanation</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="Rina might be testing the microphone inside Studio A" data-hint="Begin with Rina, use might + be + -ing for one possible current activity, then add the object and location." data-fix="Rina might be testing the microphone inside Studio A." data-success-message="Correct. Might keeps the explanation open, and be testing presents an activity possibly in progress now.">
    <h3>Build 1: preserve uncertainty</h3><p class="tile-target">Offer one unconfirmed explanation for the sound.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="microphone">microphone</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="a">A</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="might">might</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="rina">Rina</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="inside">inside</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="studio">Studio</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="testing">testing</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="be">be</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">the</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="tile-game" data-tile-builder data-answer="Rina cannot be in Studio B because the room is empty" data-hint="Begin with Rina, use cannot + base be for the rejected location, and state the checked contradiction after because." data-fix="Rina cannot be in Studio B because the room is empty." data-success-message="Correct. Cannot strongly rejects Studio B, and the because-clause makes the contradictory evidence explicit.">
    <h3>Build 2: state the contradiction</h3><p class="tile-target">Reject Studio B and include the evidence.</p>
    <div class="tile-bank" data-build-bank><button class="tile" data-build-tile="empty">empty</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="b">B</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="because">because</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="rina">Rina</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="room">room</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="cannot">cannot</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="is">is</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="in">in</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="the">the</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="studio">Studio</button><button class="tile" data-build-tile="be">be</button></div>
    <div class="build-area" data-build-area data-placeholder="Tap words in order"></div><div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-build>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-build>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Notice and repair the reasoning form</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="to" data-hint="A modal is followed directly by the base form be." data-fix="Rina must be near Studio A." data-why="Do not insert to between a modal and its base verb." data-success-message="Correct. Must connects directly to base be.">
    <h3>Repair 1: remove the infinitive marker</h3><p>Tap the word that does not belong after the modal.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="rina">Rina</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="must">must</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="to">to</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="be">be near Studio A.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="mustnt" data-hint="The checked empty room provides a strong negative deduction, not a rule that prohibits Rina from being there." data-fix="Rina can’t be in Studio B; it is empty and locked." data-why="Can’t is the clearest strong negative deduction here. Mustn’t commonly expresses prohibition in U.S. English, while full must not can be contextually ambiguous." data-success-message="Correct. Can’t rejects the location from contradictory evidence without changing the meaning into a prohibition.">
    <h3>Repair 2: distinguish deduction from prohibition</h3><p>Studio B was checked empty and locked. Tap the modal that gives the wrong core meaning for this controlled conclusion.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="rina">Rina</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="mustnt">mustn’t be</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="studio">in Studio B;</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="empty">it is empty and locked.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>
  <div class="micro-drill" data-spot-error data-answer="is" data-hint="After might, use base be before the -ing activity." data-fix="Rina might be testing the microphone." data-why="A possible activity in progress uses might + be + -ing, not might + is + -ing." data-success-message="Correct. Might be testing keeps one modal and the complete progressive base form.">
    <h3>Repair 3: build the current activity</h3><p>Tap the finite form that must return to the base form after <span class="ex">might</span>.</p><div class="spot-options"><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="rina">Rina</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="might">might</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="is">is</button><button class="error-choice" data-error-choice="testing">testing the microphone.</button></div><p class="hidden-answer" data-error-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Use it without investigating a real person</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <p class="note-line">You never need to infer anything about a real coworker, client, family member, neighbor, health condition, disability, relationship, location, schedule, finances, legal matter, identity, accent, nationality, password, message, or private behavior. Use Rina, Eli, North Hall, and the fictional evidence cards on this page. Real details are optional.</p>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Observation cards:</b> Create five neutral fictional clues about rooms, equipment, schedules, sounds, or written records. Label each observation before drawing a conclusion.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Possibilities:</b> Keep two explanations alive with <span class="ex">might</span> and <span class="ex">could</span>. Say what additional evidence would distinguish them.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Strong conclusions:</b> Use <span class="ex">must</span> only when several clues converge. Use <span class="ex">can’t</span> only when a current reliable fact directly contradicts an explanation.</div>
  <div class="prompt-card"><b>Responsible close:</b> State one uncertainty, ask one natural possibility question, and explain how you would verify rather than present an inference as fact.</div>

  <h2>Final production and check</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Final production: give a 75–90 second fictional evidence briefing</h3>
    <p>Present at least four observations about a neutral workshop mystery. Separate the observations from your conclusions. Give two live possibilities with <span class="ex">might</span> and <span class="ex">could</span>, one possible negative with <span class="ex">might not</span>, one strong positive inference with <span class="ex">must</span>, and one strong negative inference with <span class="ex">can’t</span>. Include one state and one activity in progress. End with a natural question and the evidence you would verify next.</p>
    <p><b>Evidence check:</b> Can the listener name the exact clue behind every conclusion, identify which alternatives remain live, distinguish a contradiction from missing information, recover every modal plus base-form chain, and tell which statement is observation rather than inference?</p>
  </div>
  <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You calibrated converging evidence with must, an unanswered call with might not, and a direct contradiction with can’t.">
    <h3>Final check: defend the reasoning strength</h3>
    <div class="quiz-list">
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Rina must be near Studio A." data-hint="Three recent independent clues converge on the same general area." data-fix="Rina must be near Studio A."><p class="quiz-question">1. Reception checked Rina in at 2:07, her case is beside Studio A, and staff heard a test there at 2:09. Which conclusion matches the converging evidence?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina must be near Studio A.">Rina must be near Studio A.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina can’t be anywhere in North Hall.">Rina can’t be anywhere in North Hall.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="A rule says Rina must stand beside the case.">A rule says Rina must stand beside the case.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Rina might not have her phone with her." data-hint="One unanswered call preserves several explanations and does not justify can’t." data-fix="Rina might not have her phone with her."><p class="quiz-question">2. Rina has not answered one call, and nobody has checked her phone. Which conclusion is appropriately tentative?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina might not have her phone with her.">Rina might not have her phone with her.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina can’t be available for any reason.">Rina can’t be available for any reason.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina must dislike every phone call.">Rina must dislike every phone call.</button></div></div>
      <div class="quiz-item" data-quiz-item data-answer="Rina can’t be in Studio B." data-hint="A current direct check found Studio B empty and locked." data-fix="Rina can’t be in Studio B."><p class="quiz-question">3. A staff member checked Studio B at 2:10 and found it empty and locked. Which conclusion states the contradiction?</p><div class="quiz-options"><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina can’t be in Studio B.">Rina can’t be in Studio B.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina might be visible inside Studio B.">Rina might be visible inside Studio B.</button><button class="quiz-option" data-quiz-option="Rina mustn’t enter any studio as a rule.">Rina mustn’t enter any studio as a rule.</button></div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="drill-actions"><button class="lesson-btn" data-check-quiz>Check</button><button class="lesson-btn" data-reset-quiz>Reset</button></div><p class="feedback" data-feedback></p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reflect: how strong is the evidence, not just the modal?</h2>
  <div class="lead-rule"></div>
  <div class="thinking-card">
    <h3>Explain the decision path</h3>
    <p>Complete these ideas aloud: <span class="ex">An observation is...</span> <span class="ex">An inference is...</span> <span class="ex">I use must when...</span> <span class="ex">I use can’t when...</span> <span class="ex">I keep might or could when...</span> <span class="ex">Might not differs from can’t because...</span></p>
    <p>Take one fictional clue and produce three responses: an unsupported overclaim, a calibrated possibility, and a strong conclusion after adding decisive evidence. Explain what changed in the evidence, not only in the modal.</p>
    <p><a href="#next-day-retrieval">Return tomorrow for the three-item retrieval check</a> before rereading the lesson.</p>
  </div>

  <section id="next-day-retrieval" data-lesson-extension="Next-day evidence strength, present activity form, and modal-function retrieval">
    <h2>Next-day retrieval</h2>
    <div class="lead-rule"></div>
    <p>Return without rereading the page. Name the observation, list any remaining alternatives, and identify a direct contradiction before selecting a modal.</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz data-success-message="Correct. You retained the evidence-strength distinction, the modal plus be plus -ing chain, and the difference between deduction and obligation.">
